  "meta": {
    "appVersion": "0.1.0-alpha",
    "author": "Optibench examples",
    "created": "2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z",
    "description": "Three experiments with identical 5 mW p-polarized (0 deg) HeNe lasers. Row 1 (y = 0) - Malus's law: an analyser at 60 deg passes I0 cos^2(60 deg) = 25 % (x 0.95 peak transmission). Change the angle and watch the meter follow cos^2. Row 2 (y = 120) - three-polarizer paradox: polarizers at 0 and 90 deg are crossed (no light), but inserting a third at 45 deg between them lets 1/4 of the light through (each crossing at 45 deg passes cos^2 45 = 1/2). Remove the middle one and the meter drops to ~0. Row 3 (y = 240) - a half-wave plate with its fast axis at 22.5 deg rotates the polarization by 45 deg; a polarizing beam-splitter cube transmits the p component and reflects the s component upwards, splitting the beam 50/50 between two power meters. A quarter-wave plate at 45 deg in the last stretch turns the transmitted p light circular: the final analyser at 0 deg then passes half of it regardless of its angle.",
    "dimension": "2d",
    "modified": "2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z",
    "title": "Polarization Lab"
  },
